{"name":"Druid Green","hex":"#427131","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"druid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of an order of priests among certain groups of Celts before the adoption of Abrahamic religions.","A priest or mage who uses magic based on nature or trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/druid"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Druid","blocks":[{"text":"A druid was a member of the learned class of the ancient Celtic peoples of Gaul and Britain, described by Greek and Roman writers as a priest, teacher, judge and custodian of religious and legal tradition. Because the druids committed nothing of their teaching to writing and cannot be securely identified in the archaeological record, almost everything known about them comes from outside observers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Two_Druids.PNG/330px-Two_Druids.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druid"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Gaius_Julius_Caesar_%28100-44_BC%29.JPG/330px-Gaius_Julius_Caesar_%28100-44_BC%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Gaius_Julius_Caesar_%28100-44_BC%29.JPG/330px-Gaius_Julius_Caesar_%28100-44_BC%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Julius Caesar, whose Commentarii de Bello Gallico is the earliest detailed account of the druids.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGaius_Julius_Caesar_(100-44_BC).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Romans_murdering_Druids_and_burning_their_groves_cropped.jpg/500px-Romans_murdering_Druids_and_burning_their_groves_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Romans_murdering_Druids_and_burning_their_groves_cropped.jpg/500px-Romans_murdering_Druids_and_burning_their_groves_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman troops attacking the druids on Anglesey, as described by Tacitus. A 19th-century imagining of the scene.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARomans_murdering_Druid